  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Fri Apr 17 2026\n\n...SATURDAY... \nCold front currently rolling through Roswell and Southeast New \nMexico this evening will reach El Paso's Rio Grande Valley early \nSaturday morning (around 2-4 AM tonight). Winds will shift to east\nthereafter, leading to a cooler day on Saturday with lowland \nhighs in the mid 70s. Initial winds with frontal passage don't \nlook to be anything significant, but will increase Saturday night \ninto Sunday as pressure gradients tighten due to the approach of a\n1027mb surface high over Oklahoma/Texas. \n\n...SUNDAY...\nContinued cold air advection will lead to even cooler \ntemperatures for Sunday (Lowland highs lower 70s, 5-10 degrees \nbelow normal for mid-April). More notable will be the gusty winds \nearly Sunday morning and through the daytime hours. Strongest \nwinds will favor mountain west slopes, including west El Paso. \nWouldn't be surprised to see wind gusts in the 30 to 40 mph range \nthere. More blowing dust will be possible around the Lordsburg \nPlaya along I-10 that afternoon.\n\nMoisture return up the Rio Grande valley will increase dewpoints\nand push precipitable water back up to around 0.8\" Sunday night.\nThis will lead to more cloud coverage and a very slim (10-20%)\nchance of rain showers Sunday night into Monday. This is mainly\nfor El Paso/Hudspeth Counties. Any rain that does fall will be \nvery light (<0.05\").\n\n...MONDAY AND TUESDAY...\nWarming trend early next week, with lowland highs back into the \nmid 80s by Tuesday afternoon. Ridging flow aloft will keep wind \nspeeds down over the timeframe, so minimal weather threats. \nMarginal moisture sticks around on Monday, which could lead to \nvery spotty rain over the high terrain (Sacramento Mountains, \nBlack Range) otherwise the rest of the region will be dry. \n\n...WEDNESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY... \nStrengthening southwest flow ahead of a Pacific low will bring \nmore breezy to windy conditions midweek. We may need Wind \nHeadlines depending on the eventual evolution and timing of this \nsystem. Right now, deterministic models show a Pacific low\nswinging across the Central Rockies, well to our north thus no\nprecipitation chances. Confidence is high in a warm, dry, and \nbreezy weather pattern the rest of next week.",
